From 52683f8144a0823e0f5de3857d0e299f25603a8b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Guilhem Bichot <guilhem.bichot@contributor>


Date: Sat, 22 Nov 2025 12:35:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] BUGFIX The results of the left join query are
 inconsistent.

A problem with DEFAULT(column).

https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/miscellaneous-functions.html#function_default

Considering:
CREATE TABLE t0 (c1 TINYINT);
INSERT INTO t0 VALUES(0),(1);
CREATE TABLE t98 (c3 DECIMAL(10,0) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1317787993');
(necessary details: declared NOT NULL and with a default).

We expect DEFAULT(t98.c3) to return 1317787993.
This is generally the case, but not for:

SELECT DEFAULT(t98.c3) FROM t0 LEFT JOIN t98 ON TRUE;

In Item_default_value::fix_fields(), we make a Field and point it to
the default value (s->default_values). During execution, we have
NULL-complementing for t98, so we fill table->record[0] with NULLs and
call t98->set_nullable(); then we evaluate DEFAULT, which calls
Item_field::send(), which tests Field:is_null() which returns true
because, when the field is not nullable (like here),
table->is_nullable() is used ;

Field::is_null() {
    if (is_nullable()) return (m_null_ptr[row_offset] & null_bit);
    if (is_tmp_nullable()) return m_is_tmp_null;
    return table->has_null_row();
}

(if the field had been nullable, the first if() would trigger, and as
m_null_ptr points into the default values, which are not NULL,
is_null() would have returned false; we can say that DEFAULT is
isolated from the current row of the table by having a different
record pointer, but alas not totally isolated as both share the
'table' pointer and thus share the value if table->has_null_row()).

So the SELECT returns NULL. We can say this is a bug in MySQL, I filed
it at filed https://bugs.mysql.com/bug.php?id=119453 "DEFAULT(column)
returns wrong result when column is to the right of left join"
(If the field had been nullable, the SELECT would have returned
1317787993.)

Let's now add GROUP BY 1 to our query:
SELECT DEFAULT(t98.c3) FROM t0 LEFT JOIN t98 ON TRUE GROUP BY 1;

Then left join elimination is tried on this query ;
Item_default_value::used_tables() is always 0 (which is actually
sensible as it's not really a reference: DEFAULT is not going to need
any row of the table, only return a default found in the table's
definition), so this reference goes unnoticed when we wonder if we can
eliminate the table, so we do elimination.

The consequence of elimination is a crash. Two things happen:
- there is no LEFT JOIN anymore, so there is no NULL-complementing:
Item_default_value has value 1317787993 (a good thing).
- during elimination we do: bitmap_clear_all(tl->table->read_set). The
intention is the following: if it turns out that some reference to
this eliminated table had remained somewhere unnoticed, when a read of
any column is tried from that reference it should trigger a failure of
the assertion which checks that the column is marked in read_set
(ASSERT_COLUMN_MARKED_FOR_READ). However, this breaks for our query:
this Item's evaluation, because the item is not NULL anymore,
continues into Field_new_decimal::val_decimal() which has this
assertion (in Item_default_value::fix_fields() the column was added to
read_set but join elimination cleared it afterwards). The assertion
fires.

If I remove the bitmap_clear_all() there is no more crash. But still,
the query with elimination and without do not return the same thing
(1317787993 vs NULL) because the value of DEFAULT is different.

If we fix it, then we run the risk that future tests by QA, for any
feature, will spot different-result queries when using DEFAULT, and
will require manual inspection by QA to verify that this is because of
our bugfix and not a new problem.

The proposed solution is instead to avoid the problem: if a query
contains DEFAULT, do not do elimination. We can do it in several more
or less fine-grained ways.

1. Detect if there is DEFAULT applied to a column of a weak-side
table, by walking the entire query block, as a last check before doing
elimination.

2. Add a bool property to Query_block, 'bool
m_has_items_forbidding_left_join_elimination', and set it to true in
Item_default_value::fix_fields(); also propagate this property when
merging a Query_block into a parent (like: view merging, semi-join,
etc). Do not do elimination if this bool is true.

3. Variant of 2: instead of a bool, let it be a map of the table
referenced by DEFAULT; if several DEFAULTs are used, the map is the
union. Do not do elimination for tables in this map.

4. Improve the computation of used_tables() inside the logic of
elimination: like is already done for aggregate and window functions,
do not use their "deceiving" used_tables(), instead use
argument->used_tables(); this would allow us to say "this table is
actually referenced so do not eliminate it". But, we do not have
information saying "this Item contains a DEFAULT", unlike in the case
of aggregates where we have has_aggregation(), and this would be
necessary in the lambda of CompileItem().

5. Change the code of Item_default_value::used_tables() to return the
argument's used_tables() instead of 0. This makes sense for our
problem, but not in general: as said earlier, DEFAULT does not depend
on the current row, at least in its definition (we saw it does depend
on NULL-complementing, but this is a bug). Very long ago, it did
return the argument's used_tables(), then was changed to return 0;
changing it back thus sounds risky.

1 is a bit costly (full walk). If we then find no DEFAULT, we gain a
lot of execution time thanks to the elimination, which will compensate
the costly walk; if we find a DEFAULT, we have paid the cost and get
no gain. OTOH, DEFAULT in a SELECT query sounds quite improbable in a
real-life customer query.

3 is fast, OTOH it grows Query_block by 8 bytes.

2 is fast, grows Query_block by 1 byte, or actually by 0 byte as we
can re-use bit 1<<6 in active_options(), but is coarse-grained, more
than 3 as it blocks elimination for _all_ tables.

4 is not possible, as we saw.

5 is not well-grounded in logic, and risks changing behaviour for
other queries.

The chosen solution is 2. If more problematic items are found in
further testing, the flag can be renamed to a more generic name.
